Task: produce ONE markdown mitigation playbook document, and nothing else.
The document MUST match the exact structure below. A deterministic Python
validator will reject your output if any of these constraints are violated:

- Every `##` section listed below must be present, in order, with the exact
  heading text.
- The `<!-- meta -->` block must contain the keys `slug`, `class_name`,
  `seen`, `created_from`, `last_updated`, each on its own line as `key: value`.
- Applicability, Mitigation Procedure, Post-Mitigation Verification, and
  Required Evidence must each be non-empty.
- Required Evidence must contain at least one `- [ ]` checkbox item, and
  every item there must be a `- [ ]` checkbox.
- The following vague phrases are FORBIDDEN anywhere in the document:
  "appropriate values", "as needed", "relevant configuration", "if applicable",
  "as appropriate". Use parameterized placeholders for incident-specific
  tokens.

Authoring rules specific to a v1-from-recovery mitigation playbook:
- Mitigation Procedure and Post-Mitigation Verification steps must be
  GROUNDED in the recovery_observations list, but written as the abstract
  pattern that the observation reveals. Translate each observation into a
  concrete Action / Expected outcome entry, parameterizing the specific
  resource names, env var names, and values that recovery saw with
  `<DEPLOYMENT>`, `<NAMESPACE>`, `<ENV_VAR>`, etc.
- Required Evidence items must describe the SHAPE of what recovery checked
  AFTER applying the fix (e.g. "All target Deployment pods are Running with
  restartCount=0"), not the literal pod or container name from this one
  recovery run. Each item is a `- [ ]` checkbox.
- For EVERY entry in `recovery_stage_failures`, emit one Failure Pattern
  bullet of the shape:
    - **<stage>:** "<abstracted description of the incorrect fix attempt,
      parameterized over the specific tokens>". **Oracle:** <abstracted
      correction grounded in evidence>. **Lesson:** <lesson, rewritten in
      general terms so it applies to any future incident in this class>.
- Known Pitfalls come from the original failed `stage_outputs`: identify
  patterns that looked like the right fix but were not, and describe them as
  general patterns. If there are no honest pitfalls, emit
  `- (none observed yet)` as a single bullet.
- The meta block MUST be filled exactly as:
    slug: {{ slug }}
    class_name: {{ class_name }}
    seen: 1
    created_from: recovery
    last_updated: {{ current_iso }}

